part of this video is sponsored by data  cab  today i'd like to take a look at one  american exam the sat  it's used for college admission in the  u.s and it's been around in some form  for nearly 100 years in 2020  there were over 2.2 million students who  sat the exam  you don't need to be in america to take  it it comes with its  controversies and criticism and anything  that's sat by that many students  is bound to have a cultural impact but  the sat  isn't like the extremely hard entrance  exams that we've seen from other  countries  like india and china you don't even need  to know calculus to be able to ace the  math section  it comes with a different motive instead  of trying to rank what a student has  already learned  the sat is supposed to be designed to  assess  a student's readiness for college and it  forms  only part of a college application  alongside  personal essays references school grades  and  extracurricular activities so just out  of interest  let's have a look at it i've got here a  practice test  it takes three hours excluding an  optional essay  there's a reading and writing section  and a math section  with maths there's mostly multiple  choice  and for some questions you can't use a  calculator and some you can  so here's the math questions and they do  start off pretty easy  we have finding the value of an unknown  in a simple equation  so we've got algebra some algebra again  here  at more of a word problem and working  out a linear relationship  from this table you'll want to be  confident with  reading graphs and your algebra skills  and simplification  but all of the questions are possible to  figure out in just a few lines of  working  this question uses trigonometry you just  need to find the cosine of an  angle and know that a similar triangle  will have  similar angles there's questions about  parabolas  circles and simultaneous equations  this is the section where you're allowed  to use a calculator  but to be honest you don't need it for  most of them  for a few of them though it might help  you work out the answers sooner  but for a lot of them i feel like it  would actually slow you down  this question shows two lists of numbers  and asks  if the means are the same or different  and if the standard  deviations are the same or different i  kind of like this question because  it reminds me of this part on mario  party  where you can unlock special dice but  you have to choose whether or not you  actually want to use them  i tried the questions myself under the  time pressure  and i did get too wrong because of silly  mistakes  35 was one of the ones i got wrong  you've got  a times x plus b is equal to 4x  plus 10. it says in the equation above  a and b are constants if the equation  has  infinitely many solutions for x what is  the value of b  i left my answer as just 10 over a and  didn't realize that i could solve for a  as well the other one that i got wrong  was question 14.  the square root of 4x is equal to x  minus 3.  what are all values of x that satisfy  the given equation  is it one or is it nine or is it both or  neither  i originally wrote down the right answer  for this which was b  but then i crossed it out and wrote c  because i got bamboozled over whether or  not they were talking about the  principal route  i can claim that the questions were kind  of easy for me but  i'm really not the target audience for  this i'm older than what you would be  trying to set this which i think would  be around 17.  the reading and writing questions were i  found a bit harder because there are  some questions in here that i thought  were pretty subjective  and there's definitely no room to argue  your point when you've just got a  multiple choice answer  for reading we have to read this passage  here  and then answer questions about it in  the passage there's a part where  a teacher is telling her students about  a trip that she went on  and this is described from the point of  view of one of her students  question 10 asks which sentence is the  best evidence  that the students are baffled by the  teacher's story  and can you guess what we saw from the  train miss spivvy asked  we could not camels she said  we saw a whole caravan of camels  she looked around the room waiting for  us to be amazed and delighted at the  thought  i thought that last sentence was the  answer  but i got it wrong it's actually the  sentence that comes next  we all hung there for a minute thinking  hard until mavis davis spoke up  so that's the reading section let's have  a look at the writing one  you've also got a passage to read but  then it's marked with little numbers for  each question  where it will ask you if there's a  better way to say what is underlined  with the math section at least it  doesn't seem impossible  that if you kept trying you might be  able to get close to a perfect score  and that's the interesting thing is that  you can actually take this test  i believe as many times as you want so  you can keep taking it  until you get a score that you're happy  with it's offered seven times throughout  the year  but the thing with that is that it costs  money to take  it costs around fifty dollars which does  certainly sound  unfair to people who can't afford to  just  keep taking it and then there's the cost  of things like tutors  and other preparation to try and do  better  so the sat might seem kinda easy  and it's probably not good at  distinguishing between the top students  because there are no extremely difficult  problems  the makers of the test claim that the  sat does  assess the literacy and numeracy skills  that a student would need to succeed in  college  and they also claim that the sat  combined with high school grades is a  better indicator of success in college  than high school grades alone but more  and more colleges  are making the sat optional on your  application  and it all points towards a more  holistic approach  to college applications the top schools  are still very competitive  but you need to find other ways to stand  out  rather than just performing well on a  standardized test  this section of the video is sponsored  by data camp  if you're looking for a way to further  stretch your mind  and learn some useful applications of  your skills  then check out data camp it is an online  learning platform that makes it easy to  build the skills for  analyzing data you can learn at your own  pace with  interactive courses and hands-on  exercises  there are over 300 courses that range  from beginner to advanced  for example the python data science  toolbox  has some super useful ideas for  scientists or anyone working with data  and you can do all of the activities  from within your browser  i also love the project where you learn  to draw flowers  using mathematics and the language r  in fact i think i'd like to use some of  these plots in a future video  the first chapter of every course is  free and subscription start at 25  a month there's no credit card required  to sign up  so use my link down in the description  to have a look yourself  thanks data camp and thanks to my  patreon supporters for helping me to  make videos  a special shout out to the patreon cats  of the day  kilgorean and juliet
 

